WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.720
Critics have been pretty disappointed with
Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes of Grindelwald.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.720
But don't be too sad, Potter fans, because
the film still has some really good things

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.640
going for it too.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:14.640
Here's a spoiler filled look at the best and
worst things in Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:15.640
of Grindelwald.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.580
One major new character introduced in The
Crimes of Grindelwald is Newt Scamander's

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.680
brother Theseus.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.820
An auror and war hero, when he's first introduced,
Theseus seems like he's going to be a real

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.660
stick in the mud.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.580
Instead, he proves to be a steadfast champion,
even when temporarily at cross-purposes with

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.320
his brother.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.620
We're looking forward to seeing how their
relationship develops over the next several

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.120
films.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.940
While the relationship between Newt and Theseus
is a winner, the rest of the family drama

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.800
in The Crimes of Grindelwald is a letdown.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.760
Not only do we have to muddle through Newt
and Theseus both pining over Leta LeStrange,

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:53.090
we also have the matter of her long lost half-brother
showing up, another long-lost half-brother

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:57.860
sinking down, the mystery of Credence's real
parentage, and the romantic travails of Queenie

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:58.480
and Jacob.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.780
Add in Queenie's search for her sister Tina,
and Tina's mooning over Newt and you get a

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.020
disappointing mess.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.340
On the other hand, Jude Law's portrayal of
Albus Dumbledore in his prime is a complete

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.720
win, and arguably the best part of the film.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.180
Every time he's on screen, the whole movie
comes alive, and when he's offscreen, sadness

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.340
and woe fill the heart.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.060
And there really should be more of him: despite
the pretense that Newt is the main character,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:25.889
make no mistake, this series is really about
Dumbledore and Grindelwald.

00:01:25.889 --> 00:01:29.289
It's disappointing that the film doesn't get
into their relationship more, but hopefully

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:31.960
we'll get a whole lot more of Dumbledore in
the next installment.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.880
One of the biggest problems with the movie,
in fact, is that instead of showing us more

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.880
Newt and Dumbledore, it insists on spending
too much time with unimportant supporting

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:41.380
characters.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.280
That's why we get diversions like Newt's assistant
Bunty, who is so minor even Newt spends her

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.160
entire scene telling her to go away.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.900
The film saves its worst treatment, though,
for poor Nagini.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.280
In the original Harry Potter series, Nagini
was Voldemort's murderous pet snake.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.399
In The Crimes of Grindelwald, though, it's
revealed that Nagini was originally a human

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.859
woman cursed to eventually turn into a snake.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.400
It could be a compelling story but it's a
story The Crimes of Grindelwald isn't interested

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.060
in exploring.

00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:12.740
Instead, Nagini is basically a living Easter
egg, treated as little more than a prop to

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:15.500
add flavor to Credence's equally boring character
arc.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.760
Why even bother?

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.300
It might be all too brief, but those sweeping
shots of Hogwarts in all its pre-Wizarding

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.400
War glory feel like a warm dip into the pensieve.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.390
The return to the school's grounds creates
an Easter egg extravaganza for Potter fans,

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:30.880
sure.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:36.140
But more importantly, seeing the cozy familiarity
of the Great Hall or the stone bridge just

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.440
feels like coming home.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:42.020
It's too bad Hogwarts doesn't serve as the
central backdrop for the film New York and

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:46.870
Paris are both lovely enough, but Hogwarts
is simply spellbinding to return to.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.920
"should things at some point go terribly wrong,
it's good to have a place to go"

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:56.040
In Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them,
Queenie is quirky, but her charisma and unexpected

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.000
adulation of the ordinary no-maj Jacob only
makes her more charming.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:04.340
In The Crimes of Grindelwald, though, she's
morphed into a clingy and petulant person

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:09.240
who abandons both her values and her sensibilities
by throwing in with Grindelwald for reasons

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.400
that make absolutely no sense.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.740
It's a major letdown from the cheerfully enigmatic
person we were introduced to in the first

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:16.240
film.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.940
One of the most fun things about Fantastic
Beasts and Where to Find Them was the introduction

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.620
of some new mythical creatures.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:28.120
The same is true in Crimes of Grindelwald,
where Newt encounters a handful of new beasts,

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.760
including the awesome Zouwu , which he tames
with a simple cat toy.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.060
Best of all, though, is the return of the
niffler, which stole the spotlight in Fantastic

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:38.140
Beasts and Where to Find Them.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.410
The inclusion of the niffler isn't just a
cute callback, though, it's a key plot point,

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:46.280
as the niffler helps Newt literally change
the course of history thanks to some well

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:47.200
timed thievery.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.420
There's some history that apparently even
the niffler can't change, though, and unfortunately

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.920
we saw way too much of it in The Crimes of
Grindelwald.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:59.720
During a key sequence, Grindelwald presents
a vision of the future to his rapt followers,

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.260
previewing the horrors that await them in
World War II.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:06.480
That's all fine, but director David Yates
took things too far by showing footage of

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.270
Holocaust victims being marched into death
camps.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:13.510
Invoking the Holocaust was not only unnecessary,
it went beyond the bounds of good taste.

